            <ArticleTitle>Mid-infrared wavelength conversion from As&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;Se&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt; microwires.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>639-642</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1364/OL.42.000639</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We demonstrate all-fiber far-detuned and widely tunable mid-infrared wavelength conversion using As&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;Se&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt; microwires. In a first experiment, an idler is generated and tuned from 2.351 to &gt;2.500 μm from four-wave mixing in a 0.5 cm long microwire. In a second experiment, tunable parametric sidebands are generated via modulation instability in a 10 cm long microwire. The resulting parametric frequency conversion reaches up to 49.3 THz, the largest ever reported in soft glass materials.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
